      "text_raw": "When the series resistor in\nthe Source is greatly\nincreased, i.e. when an ideal\ncurrent source I is used\nB\nwith infinite output resis-\ntance, then the distortion\ndue to the non-linearity of\nthe I −V characteristic\nDS GS\nis zero.\nIn this case, the non-lin-\nearity of the output conduc-\ntance is dominant. It can\nagain be described in terms\nof relative current swing U,\nas given in this slide. Voltage\nV L is the Early voltage of\nE\nthe MOST. The larger the Early voltage, the smaller the relative current swing and the smaller\nthe distortion is.\nThe same applies to an emitter follower.\nWe have silently assumed that the Bulk-Source voltage of the source follower is zero. This is",
      "text": "When the series resistor in the Source is greatly increased, i.e. when an ideal current source I is used B with infinite output resistance, then the distortion due to the non-linearity of the I −V characteristic DS GS is zero. In this case, the non-linearity of the output conductance is dominant. It can again be described in terms of relative current swing U, as given in this slide. Voltage V L is the Early voltage of E the MOST. The larger the Early voltage, the smaller the relative current swing and the smaller the distortion is. The same applies to an emitter follower. We have silently assumed that the Bulk-Source voltage of the source follower is zero. This is"
